    "narrative": "Our lineup construction for today leans into favorable matchups against vulnerable arms, with **Taylor Ward** headlining as our strongest play (71.19). **Taylor Ward** benefits from a massive park factor boost and a significant BvP historical edge, allowing him to overcome a mediocre matchup against Framber Valdez. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** (67.01) is a prime target against Lucas Giolito; the combination of the pitcher\u2019s poor 5.01 xERA and a strong dynamic platoon advantage makes him a high-upside asset despite being buried at the bottom of the order. **Bryson Stott** (65.11) and **Tyler Freeman** (62.30) also provide elite value, with **Bryson Stott** specifically highlighted for his potential to exploit an easy SB opportunity against the slow delivery of Slade Cecconi.\n\nWhile the core is solid, we are forced to navigate several tough matchups against top-tier pitching. **Daulton Varsho** (50.42) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (52.07) both face elite arms in Skenes and Wheeler, respectively. These scores are suppressed significantly by the opposing SP quality, but their categorical contributions\u2014particularly the stolen base potential in a scarcity-driven market\u2014keep them active. The most difficult bench decision was **Gavin Sheets** (49.29), who trailed **Jeremiah Jackson** (54.32) due to the latter\u2019s superior park factors and the optimizer\u2019s preference for **Jeremiah Jackson**\u2019s offensive environment. We remain mindful that with several lineups still pending, these projections are subject to drift; **Will Smith**, **Luis Arraez**, and **Matt Chapman** are all currently assuming lineup spots that, if altered, could shift our efficiency scores downward. We are effectively managing our game caps by keeping **Drew Gilbert** on the pine, while **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz** remain sidelined on the IL, leaving our catching depth thin.",
